  "plan_notes": "The Prince and the Pauper is a voice-driven satirical novel in which the narrator's ironic commentary is as important as the plot. The two main dangers in compression are: (1) losing Twain's satirical asides, which are often single sentences inserted mid-paragraph and easily cut by mechanical compression; (2) losing the structural symmetry between the Tom-at-court and Edward-in-the-world arcs, which must remain interleaved and proportionally balanced in the condensed version.\n\nREGISTER: The novel is written in deliberate mock-Tudor idiom — 'prithee,' 'dost,' 'forsooth,' 'an' it please thee' — which is Twain's own choice, not historical accuracy. This register must be preserved, especially in dialogue. Modernising the Tudor speech would destroy the comedy of the gap between Tom's Offal Court origins and his courtly speech, and would eliminate the irony of a narrator speaking modern American English about characters speaking 16th-century English.\n\nSATIRICAL ASIDES: Many of the book's most important passages are not in the main narrative stream but in narrator intrusions: 'Why they did not use a dog or a plumber seems strange; but all the ways of royalty are strange.' These are easy to cut as 'decoration' but are actually the book's primary satirical vehicle. Every such aside should be flagged as a signature and treated as non-negotiable.\n\nSTRUCTURAL SYMMETRY: The book alternates between Tom's life at court (Chapters 5-8, 14-16, 30-32) and Edward's life in the world (Chapters 4, 10, 17-29). In a compression, the temptation is to cut the Edward-in-the-world sections more heavily because they are more episodic. Resist this: Yokel's speech, the women burned at the stake, the knife-whetting hermit, and the calf scene are all Edward-arc material and are among the novel's most important passages.\n\nCHRONOLOGY CAUTION: The novel is set in early 1547 (January-February). The coronation is February 20. Henry VIII dies during the story. These dates are important for the legal/ceremonial context but need not be foregrounded in a condensed version.\n\nDIGRESSIONS: Twain has several extended digressions that are partially thematically relevant (London Bridge as social satire, Christ's Hospital history) and partially pure voice-essay material. These should be significantly compressed but not eliminated — a 3-4 sentence sample of the best material is better than nothing. The footnotes/notes section (Chapter 035) is entirely extradiegetic and should be excluded.\n\nKEY PAYOFFS TO PRESERVE IN FINAL CHAPTERS: (1) Miles's right to sit (planted Ch. 12, paid off Ch. 33). (2) The Great Seal hidden in Milanese armour (planted Ch. 3, tested Ch. 32, resolved Ch. 32). (3) Mrs. Canty's hand-gesture recognition test (planted Ch. 10, paid off Ch. 31). (4) Tom's natural judicial wisdom (demonstrated Ch. 15, validated Ch. 33). All four of these structural payoffs require their setups to be legible in the compressed version."
